{:edb/db-before-t 1106 :edb/db-after-t 1107 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534419 50 #inst "2026-10-07T02:19:06.440Z" 13194139534419 true] [17592186151916 1100 "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1$, as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/e4dfe9db8587b4dfa4fae9c4610106a3.png)" 13194139534419 false] [17592186151916 1100 "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" 13194139534419 true] [17592186151916 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186151916 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n$x= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0)$.\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n$x= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$" 13194139534419 false] [17592186151917 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186151917 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n$x= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$." 13194139534419 false] [17592186151917 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta$. We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 4\\cos \\theta  \\\\\n\\frac{x}{4} &= \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4^{2}} &= \\cos^{2} \\theta  \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{16} &= \\cos^{2} \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}y + 1 & = 2 \\sin   \\theta \\\\ \\frac{y + 1}{2} & = \\sin   \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( y + 1 {)}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & = {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( y + 1 {)}^{2}}{4} & = {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  .\\end{array}$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} + \\frac{( y + 1 {)}^{2}}{4} & = {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  = 1 .\\end{array}$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1\n$$" 13194139534419 false] [17592186151917 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186151918 1100 "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4$, as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/ad11664b2d980183e25e80a1d5c231cd.png)" 13194139534419 false] [17592186151918 1100 "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" 13194139534419 true] [17592186151918 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186151918 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$x= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k)$.\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2)$, $a=6$ and $b=4$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n$x=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).$" 13194139534419 false] [17592186151919 1100 "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/83eed01dabcfcec70e64963213c46397.png)" 13194139534419 false] [17592186151919 1100 "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" 13194139534419 true] [17592186151919 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellips