, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287339 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287340 13194139534419 true] [17592186287339 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534419 true] [17592186287339 1041 "field-1" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287339 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287340 13194139534419 true] [17592186287339 1110 #uuid "3e675469-7ed3-50ca-b8c4-63856f23fd96" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287340 1034 "6\\cos(\\theta)" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287340 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534419 true] [17592186287340 1111 #uuid "3bf77ad8-e90f-5318-8684-5d94c505e0ae" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287341 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287342 13194139534419 true] [17592186287341 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534419 true] [17592186287341 1041 "field-2" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287341 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287342 13194139534419 true] [17592186287341 1110 #uuid "0c750959-1bdb-5f24-93a8-046ad41f914d" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287342 1034 "4\\sin(\\theta)" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287342 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534419 true] [17592186287342 1111 #uuid "775dbfe2-7347-5fb3-9641-24c4c41065e9" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287343 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287344 13194139534419 true] [17592186287343 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287347 13194139534419 true] [17592186287343 1089 "q-327171" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287343 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534419 true] [17592186287343 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/61ffc8879681a1d0bb381f32178ea2e7b58e251341b2280ba607448231458dc5.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(0,5)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $8,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,5).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287343 1113 #uuid "ca5b2e3e-ddb1-5792-98f6-dd4e9edd115c" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287343 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(0,5),$ $a=3$ and $b=8.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287345 13194139534419 true] [17592186287344 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287346 13194139534419 true] [17592186287344 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534419 true] [17592186287344 1041 "field-1" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287344 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287345 13194139534419 true] [17592186287344 1110 #uuid "2071a2f6-650b-5c57-86b4-17df74f93151" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287345 1034 "3\\cos(\\theta)" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287345 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534419 true] [17592186287345 1111 #uuid "24554dd2-c7a7-5795-ace8-ef8ee8123e65" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287346 1034 "8\\cos(\\theta)" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534419 true] [17592186287346 1111 #uuid "5a761898-639e-58fc-b2fd-c7718ff280db" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287347 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287348 13194139534419 true] [17592186287347 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534419 true] [17592186287347 1041 "field-2" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287347 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287348 13194139534419 true] [17592186287347 1110 #uuid "9f6c69d3-7405-5114-a936-18767e95e99d" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287348 1034 "8\\sin(\\theta)+5" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287348 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534419 true] [17592186287348 1111 #uuid "66173e3f-74f3-53c0-ad15-c0b28d4d1d6a" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287349 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287350 13194139534419 true] [17592186287349 1089 "q-327798" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287349 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534419 true] [17592186287349 1100 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287349 1113 #uuid "cb4d9250-45c7-5bda-a4ab-0665efa6965b" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287349 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{y+5}{2\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}=1.\n$$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287351 13194139534419 true] [17592186287350 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534419 true] [17592186287350 1041 "field-1" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287350 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287351 13194139534419 true] [17592186287350 1110 #uuid "b9fc26bb-45f2-5d13-ae66-63e9435e150f" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287351 1034 "\\frac{(x-3)^2}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^2}{28}" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287351 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534419 true] [17592186287351 1111 #uuid "0e65ad3f-33a1-5728-8f94-9e0e77580247" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287352 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287353 13194139534419 true] [17592186287352 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287355 13194139534419 true] [17592186287352 1089 "q-327828" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287352 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534419 true] [17592186287352 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/f29c8db431035fda7e50771b3baaf6d19f51a60e26582957bbff0f70dbfdc5ed.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287352 1113 #uuid "c27113d8-cd16-5325-957c-c8e1f76bc388" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287352 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/h