er-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287357 1113 #uuid "e133a16b-9ecd-5361-9999-9f87f8923385" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287357 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6efc63426eae95430b0b5ddee44ab1523f69eb00ba26720374f7246712f7088a.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse in the **fourth quadrant** (where $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0$).\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\np=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287358 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287359 13194139534419 true] [17592186287358 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534419 true] [17592186287358 1041 "field-1" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287358 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287359 13194139534419 true] [17592186287358 1110 #uuid "bef4d640-8597-50d3-9d30-b825413b0c9c" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287359 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534419 true] [17592186287359 1111 #uuid "51189449-f227-5e1a-9f8b-e99415d51677" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287361 13194139534419 true] [17592186287360 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534419 true] [17592186287360 1041 "field-2" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287360 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287361 13194139534419 true] [17592186287360 1110 #uuid "7723465a-3aea-5cbf-9626-b5e7cdcb2e4f" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287361 1034 "2\\pi" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534419 true] [17592186287361 1111 #uuid "ad3b1d9f-8d38-5de4-8726-2a77949f7971" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287362 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287363 13194139534419 true] [17592186287362 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287366 13194139534419 true] [17592186287362 1089 "q-327847" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287362 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534419 true] [17592186287362 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/ab9ef90cf863fee6776667a967d5939ba3564564d5d7626e07aaa868baea6a87.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287362 1113 #uuid "52cf0e11-08b0-5661-87e0-6eb45872f824" 13194139534419 true] [17592186287362 1145 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,1),$ with $a=4$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{s